<edge source="us_domestic_politics::executive_stability" target="ai_infra_market_risk::market_liquidity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">A sharp deterioration in executive stability (e.g., sudden leadership crisis or paralysis) raises near‑term policy and fiscal uncertainty, prompting market‑makers and banks to pull back inventory and widen bid‑ask spreads, which reduces market liquidity.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_domestic_politics</data>
  <data key="d40">ai_infra_market_risk</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_domestic_politics::executive_stability" target="iran_regional_escalation::military_engagement_inte" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">A loss of executive steadiness (internal factionalism, rapid personnel turnover, or poor interagency coordination) raises the risk of miscommunication, conflicting orders, or poorly planned kinetic actions that provoke Iranian retaliation and increase regional military engagement intensity.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_domestic_politics</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_regional_escalation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_domestic_politics::executive_stability" target="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_tightness" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Greater operational stability in the U.S. executive branch speeds interagency sanction design, legal review and diplomatic coordination, enabling more frequent and legally robust sanction packages to be issued and enforced.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_domestic_politics</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_domestic_politics::executive_stability" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::grid_power_availability" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Reduced stability and coordination in the executive branch hampers timely federal decision-making and emergency support for grid resilience projects and interagency responses, delaying capacity upgrades and temporarily lowering reliable power availability for data-centre operations.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_domestic_politics</data>
